"Journal of Mathematical Extension (https://ijmex.com/index.php/ijmex/article/download/419/283)\nciteturn1search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.7 years ago; Crawled: 9.9 years ago; # Domination Polynomial of Generalized Book Graphs ... Jahari ... In this paper, we study the domination polynomials of some generalized book graphs.\nJournal of Mathematical Extension  \nVol. 10, No. 3, (2016), 35-45  \nISSN: 1735-8299  \nURL: http://www.ijmex.com  \n\n# Domination Polynomial of Generalized Book Graphs\n\nS. Jahari  \nYazd University\n\nS. Alikhani*  \nYazd University\n\n**Abstract.** Let \\(G\\) be a simple graph of order \\(n\\). The domination polynomial of \\(G\\) is the polynomial \\(D(G, x)=\\sum_{i=0}^{n} d(G, i)x^{i}\\), where \\(d(G, i)\\) is the number of dominating sets of \\(G\\) of size \\(i\\). Let \\(n\\) be any positive integer and \\(B_n\\) be the \\(n\\)-book graphs, formed by joining \\(n\\) copies of the cycle graph \\(C_4\\) with a common edge. In this paper, we study the domination polynomials of some generalized book graphs. In particular we examine the domination roots of these families, and find the limiting curve for the roots.\n\n**AMS Subject Classification:** 05C60  \n**Keywords and Phrases:** Domination polynomial, generalized book graphs, family\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nLet \\(G=(V,E)\\) be a simple graph. For any vertex \\(v \\in V(G)\\), the open neighborhood of \\(v\\) is the set \\(N(v)=\\{u \\in V(G)\\mid \\{u,v\\} \\in E(G)\\}\\) and the closed neighborhood of \\(v\\) is the set \\(N[v]=N(v)\\cup\\{v\\}\\). For a set \\(S \\subseteq V(G)\\), the open neighborhood of \\(S\\) is \\(N(S)=\\bigcup_{v\\in S} N(v)\\) and the closed neighborhood of \\(S\\) is \\(N[S]=N(S)\\cup S\\). A set \\(S \\subseteq V(G)\\)\n\nReceived: August 2015. Accepted: December 2015  \n*Corresponding author\n\n35--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nJournal of Mathematical Extension (https://www.ijmex.com/index.php/ijmex/article/download/419/283/0)\nciteturn1search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.9 years ago; Jahari ... domination polynomials of some generalized book graphs. ... Keywords and Phrases: Domination polynomial, generalized book\nJournal of Mathematical Extension\nVol. 10, No. 3, (2016), 35-45\nISSN: 1735-8299\nURL: http://www.ijmex.com\nJournal of Mathematical Extension\nVol. 10, No. 3, (2016), 35-45\nISSN: 1735-8299\nURL: http://www.ijmex.com\nDomination Polynomial of Generalized\nBook Graphs\nS. Jahari\nYazd University\nS. Alikhani∗\nYazd University\nAbstract. Let G be a simple graph of order n. The domination poly-\nnomial of G is the polynomial D(G, x) = n\ni=0 d(G, i)xi, where d(G, i)\nis the number of dominating sets of G of size i. Let n be any positive\ninteger and Bn be the n-book graphs, formed by joining n copies of\nthe cycle graph C4 with a common edge. In this paper, we study the\ndomination polynomials of some generalized book graphs. In particular\nwe examine the domination roots of these families, and find the limiting\ncurve for the roots.\nAMS Subject Classification: 05C60\nKeywords and Phrases: Domination polynomial, generalized book\ngraphs, family\n1.\nIntroduction\nLet G = (V, E) be a simple graph. For any vertex v ∈V (G), the open\nneighborhood of v is the set N(v) = {u ∈V (G)|{u, v} ∈E(G)} and\nthe closed neighborhood of v is the set N[v] = N(v) ∪{v}. For a set\nS ⊆V (G), the open neighborhood of S is N(S) = \nv∈S N(v) and\nthe closed neighborhood of S is N[S] = N(S) ∪S. A set S ⊆V (G)\nReceived: August 2015; Accepted: December 2015\n∗Corresponding author\n35--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nJournal of Mathematical Extension (https://www.ijmex.com/index.php/ijmex/article/download/419/283)\nciteturn1search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; JAHARI AND S. ... This graph polynomial was introduced in the paper [5] ... roots of domination polynomial of generalized book graphs in Section 3.\n36\nS. JAHARI AND S. ALIKHANI\nis a dominating set if N[S] = V . The domination number γ(G) is\nthe minimum cardinality of a dominating set in G. Let D(G, i) be\nthe family of dominating sets of graph G with cardinality i and let\nd(G, i) = |D(G, i)|. The domination polynomial D(G, x) of G is defined\nas D(G, x) = |V (G)|\ni=γ(G) d(G, i)xi, where γ(G) is the domination number\nof G (see [1, 8]). This graph polynomial was introduced in the paper [5]\nthat appeared in 2014 but numerous other papers on the polynomial\nappeared earlier. For some very recent developments on the polynomial\nsee for example [4, 6].\nA root of D(G, x) is called a domination root of G. The set of distinct\nroots of D(G, x) is denoted by Z(D(G, x)). Calculating the domination\npolynomial of a graph G is difficult in general, as the smallest power\nof a non-zero term is the domination number γ(G) of the graph, and\ndetermining whether γ(G) k is known to be NP-complete [9]. But for\ncertain classes of graphs, we can find a closed form expression for the\ndomination polynomial.\nIn the next section, we consider generalized book graphs and compute\ntheir domination polynomials. Also we explore the nature and location of\nroots of domination polynomial of generalized book graphs in Section 3.\n2.\nDomination Polynomial of Generalized Book\nGraphs\nA book graph Bn, is defined as follows V (Bn) = {u1, u2} ∪{vi, wi : 1 \ni n} and E(Bn) = {u1u2} ∪{u1vi, u2wi, viwi : 1 i n}. We\nconsider the generalized book graph Bn,m with vertex and edge sets\nby V (Bn,m) = {ui : 1 i m −2} ∪{vi, wi : 1 i n} and\nE(Bn,m) = {uiui+1 : 1 i m −3} ∪{uiwj : 1 j n, i =\nm −2} ∪{u1vi : 1 i n} ∪{viwi : 1 i n} (see Figure 1).\nWe need some preliminaries to study the domination polynomial of some\ngeneralized book graphs. The vertex contraction G/u of a graph G by\na vertex u is the operation under which all vertices in N(u) are joined\nto each other and then u is deleted (see [12]). The following theorem is--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDomination polynomial of generalized friendship and generalized book graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.05856)\nciteturn1academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.8 years ago; Title: Domination polynomial of generalized friendship and generalized book graphs\nAuthors: Somayeh Jahari, Saeid Alikhani ... We also consider book graphs formed by joining n copies of the cycle graph of order 4 with a common edge and study the domination polynomials of some generalized book graphs.\nTitle: Domination polynomial of generalized friendship and generalized book graphs\nAuthors: Somayeh Jahari, Saeid Alikhani\nDate: Fri Jan 23 16:16:32 2015\n\nLet G be a simple graph of order n. The domination polynomial of a graph is the generating function of its dominating sets. We study the domination polynomials of generalized friendship graphs. We also consider book graphs formed by joining n copies of the cycle graph of order 4 with a common edge and study the domination polynomials of some generalized book graphs. In particular we examine the domination roots of these families and find the limiting curve for the roots.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIndependent domination polynomial of graphs related to paths (https://8ecm.eu/system/admin/abstracts/presentations/000/000/361/original/8ECM-Alikahni2021.pdf?1626101853=)\nciteturn1search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.4 years ago; Crawled: 3 days ago; Jahari and S. ... The independent domination polynomial of the generalized book graph, \\(B_{n,m}\\), for \\(n\\geq 2,\\ m\\geq 3\\) is given by ... The independent domination number of the generalized book graph, \\(B_{n,m}\\), for \\(n\\geq 2,\\ m\\geq 3\\) equals\nIndependent domination polynomial of graphs related to paths\n\n### Theorem (S. Jahari and S. A., 2021)\n\nThe independent domination polynomial of the generalized book graph, \\(B_{n,m}\\), for \\(n\\geq 2,\\ m\\geq 3\\) is given by\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(2^n-2)x^nD_i(P_{m-4},x)\n+2x^{n+1}D_i(P_{m-5},x)\\\\\n&\\qquad +(x^2+2x^{n+1})D_i(P_{m-6},x),\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nwhere for all \\(j\\leq 0\\),\n\n\\[\nD_i(P_j,x)=1.\n\\]\n\n### Theorem (S. Jahari and S. A., 2021)\n\nThe independent domination number of the generalized book graph, \\(B_{n,m}\\), for \\(n\\geq 2,\\ m\\geq 3\\) equals\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\gamma_i(B_{n,m})=\\min\\{&\n\\max\\{n,\\ n+\\lfloor\\tfrac{m-4}{2}\\rfloor\\},\\\\\n&\\max\\{n+1,\\ n+1+\\lfloor\\tfrac{m-5}{2}\\rfloor\\},\\\\\n&\\max\\{2,\\ 2+\\lfloor\\tfrac{m-6}{2}\\rfloor\\}\n\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n---\n\nSaied Alikhani (Yazd University) | Some new results on independent domination | 8th European Congress of Mathematics | 45--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the roots of independent domination polynomials of graphs (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0166218X26001587)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; Jahari and Alikhani [8] established fundamental properties of the independent domination polynomial of the generalized book graphs and generalized friendship graphs. This paper investigates the limits of roots of independent domination polynomials for these two graph families, presenting new results on their asymptotic distributions.\n\n## Discrete Applied Mathematics\n\nVolume 386, 15 June 2026, Pages 373-380\n\n# On the roots of independent domination polynomials of graphs\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.dam.2026.03.019Get rights and content\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe independent domination polynomial of a graph G$G$ is {D}_{i}(G,x)=\\sum _{k={\\gamma }_{i}\\left(G\\right)}^{\\alpha \\left(G\\right)}{d}_{i}(G,k){x}^{k},${D}_{i}(G,x)=\\sum _{k={\\gamma }_{i}\\left(G\\right)}^{\\alpha \\left(G\\right)}{d}_{i}(G,k){x}^{k},$where {d}_{i}(G,k)${d}_{i}(G,k)$ counts the independent dominating sets of size k$k$, \\alpha \\left(G\\right)$\\alpha \\left(G\\right)$ is the independence number, and {\\gamma }_{i}\\left(G\\right)${\\gamma }_{i}\\left(G\\right)$ is the independent domination number. We investigate limit curves for the roots of these polynomials for book graphs, generalized book graphs, friendship graphs, and generalized friendship graphs. Specifically, we prove that for generalized book graphs, the limit curves are x=0$x=0$ and \\left|x\\right|=\\frac{1}{2}$\\left|x\\right|=\\frac{1}{2}$, while for generalized friendship graphs, the limit curves comprise x=0$x=0$ and curves determined by the independent domination polynomials of paths.\n\n## Introduction\n\nLet G=(V,E)$G=(V,E)$ be a finite and simple graph. For a set S\\subseteq V$S\\subseteq V$, the subgraph induced by S$S$ is denoted by 〈S〉$〈S〉$, the open neighborhood of S$S$ is {N}_{G}\\left(S\\right)={\\bigcup }_{v\\in S}\\{u\\in V\\mid uv\\in E\\}${N}_{G}\\left(S\\right)={\\bigcup }_{v\\in S}\\{u\\in V\\mid uv\\in E\\}$ and the closed neighborhood of S$S$ is {N}_{G}\\left[S\\right]={N}_{G}\\left(S\\right)\\cup S${N}_{G}\\left[S\\right]={N}_{G}\\left(S\\right)\\cup S$.\n\nThe roots of various graph polynomials have garnered significant interest, both for their intrinsic value and for the insights they provide into the polynomials’ properties. Extensive studies have been conducted on the roots of chromatic, independence, domination, and total domination polynomials [1], [2], [4], [5], [6], [7], [9], [10]. For instance, Brown and Tufts [6] have shown that the closure of the domination roots is the entire complex plane. Jahari and Alikhani [8] established fundamental properties of the independent domination polynomial of the generalized book graphs and generalized friendship graphs. This paper investigates the limits of roots of independent domination polynomials for these two graph families, presenting new results on their asymptotic distributions.\n\nGiven two integers n>2$n>2$ and m>4$m>4$, a book graph {B}_{n}${B}_{n}$ (see Fig. 1) is defined as follows:\n\n  * •\n\nVertex set: V\\left({B}_{n}\\right)=\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\}\\cup \\{{v}_{i},{w}_{i}\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}$V\\left({B}_{n}\\right)=\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\}\\cup \\{{v}_{i},{w}_{i}\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}$.\n\n  * •\n\nEdge set: E\\left({B}_{n}\\right)=\\left\\{{u}_{1}{u}_{2}\\right\\}\\cup \\{{u}_{1}{v}_{i},{u}_{2}{w}_{i},{v}_{i}{w}_{i}\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}$E\\left({B}_{n}\\right)=\\left\\{{u}_{1}{u}_{2}\\right\\}\\cup \\{{u}_{1}{v}_{i},{u}_{2}{w}_{i},{v}_{i}{w}_{i}\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}$.\n\nWe consider the generalized book graph {B}_{n,m}${B}_{n,m}$ with:\n\n  * •\n\nVertex set: V\\left({B}_{n,m}\\right)=\\{{u}_{i}\\mid 1\\le i\\le m-2\\}\\cup \\{{v}_{j},{w}_{j}\\mid 1\\le j\\le n\\}$V\\left({B}_{n,m}\\right)=\\{{u}_{i}\\mid 1\\le i\\le m-2\\}\\cup \\{{v}_{j},{w}_{j}\\mid 1\\le j\\le n\\}$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDomination polynomial of generalized book graphs (https://www.academia.edu/68609916/Domination_polynomial_of_generalized_book_graphs)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; Domination Polynomial of Generalized Book Graphs A book graph Bn , is defined as follows V (Bn ) = {u1 , u2 } ∪ {vi , wi : 1  i  n} and E(Bn ) = {u1 u2 } ∪ {u1 vi , u2 wi , vi wi : 1  i  n}. ... The following theorem is ���������� ���������� �� �������������� �� ���������� ���������� �� �������������� �� ������ ��� ������ ��� ���������� ��������� ��� ��� ���������� ����������� ������ ��� ������ ��� ���������� ��������� ��� ��� ���������� ����������� ���������� ���������� �� ����������� ���� ������ �� ������� � �� ������� � ������ �� ������ �� ��� ���� � ������������� ������ �� ������ � ��� ���� � ������������� ������ �� ������ ��� ��� ���� � ������������� ������� �������������� ��� ��� ���� ������� ���� ���������� ���� ������� �� � ��������� ����������� ��� ��� ��� ������ �� � ������ ��� ������ ��� � �� � �� ���� ������� ������ � �� �� ���� ���� ���� ��